ice owns each customer communication channel: email (Amazon SES), and WhatsApp, SMS, MMS, RCS, voice and mobile push (the AWS End User Messaging family of services). Routes the request to the specialized skill for that channel. Defers to the channel's specialized skill when the user already named a specific channel. In general, use specific skills or documentation searches for detailed service-specific questions. Do NOT use for MSK or Managed Service for Apache Flink questions, prefer specific skills. Does not configure customer communication channels; defers to specific skills.\n\n\nServices: sqs, sns, eventbridge, mq, kinesis, kds, firehose, flink, msf, msk, kafka, ses, socialmessaging, pinpoint-sms-voice-v2\nTasks: learn, architect, debug, deploy\nPersona: developer, architect, devops\nWorkload: serverless, event-driven, streaming, data-analytics, messaging\n*","skill_name":"aws-messaging-and-streaming"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"managing-data-asset-access","skill_description":"Grants, requests, audits and removes access to data assets in an AWS data catalog (the Amazon SageMaker Catalog supported by Amazon DataZone) through catalog subscriptions over published listings. Covers requesting a subscription, approving or rejecting requests with a recorded decision, sharing a listing with a project, revoking one or many subscriptions, and confirming grant settlement. Triggers on: give another team access to a table, request access to a dataset, approve or reject an access request, share a listing with a project, who has access to this table, revoke access, bulk revoke, is the access actually gone, subscription grant status. Not for: IAM roles, policies or STS (use aws-iam), S3 bucket policies (use securing-s3-buckets), Clean Rooms permissions (use aws-cleanrooms), or writing catalog metadata, glossaries and forms, publishing listings, or retiring or deleting an asset that other teams are subscribed to and the order of operations that needs (use managing-data-catalog)\n\n\nServices: datazone\nTasks: deploy, audit\nPersona: data-engineer, developer, architect\nWorkload: data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"managing-data-asset-access"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"AWS expands Resource Control Policies support to Amazon DynamoDB - AWS","context":"AWS Resource Control Policies (RCPs), a type of organization policy used to manage permissions in an AWS environment, now support Amazon DynamoDB. RCPs enable central control over the maximum available permissions for resources in an organization. With this expansion, users can manage DynamoDB permissions using RCPs, preventing identities outside the organization from accessing it and enforcing baseline security standards. RCPs are available in all AWS commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. For more information on RCPs and the list of supported AWS services, visit the Resource control policies (RCPs) documentation in the AWS Organizations User Guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/02/aws-expands-resource-control-policies-amazon/"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Check AWS docs on AmazonRekognitionServiceRole SNS publish permissions scope."}

